of $f(x)/g(x)$?","options":["$\\lim f(x)/g(x)=0$","$\\lim f(x)/g(x)=+\\infty$","$\\lim f(x)/g(x)$ must be $1$","The rule cannot ever apply to $\\infty/\\infty$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"34ee953a9dec649d8894177b","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_lhopitals_rule_855cbba0_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition is one of the hypotheses of L'Hôpital's rule for $\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$?","options":["$g'(x)\\neq0$ for all $x\\neq a$ sufficiently near $a$","$f(a)=g(a)=0$","$f$ and $g$ are differentiable at the point $a$ itself","$g(x)>0$ for all $x$ sufficiently near $a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"572479e19dc3665fa60e8fa4","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_lhopitals_rule_855cbba0_006_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the statement of L'Hôpital's rule, what may the value $L$ be?","options":["Only a positive real number","Only $0$","Only a finite nonzero real number","An element of $[-\\infty,+\\infty]$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0fd7124786cafcc75fa3b897","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_lhopitals_rule_855cbba0_009_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why may L'Hôpital's rule not be used to justify $\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}=1$?","options":["Proving $(\\sin x)'=\\cos x$ already uses this limit.","The quotient $\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$ is not of the form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at $0$.","The derivative quotient $\\frac{\\cos x}{1}$ has no limit as $x\\to0$.","$\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$ is undefined at $x=0$, so the rule cannot be applied there."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0be404276c2f5db6a50a40d3","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_lhopitals_rule_855cbba0_012_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"At $a=0$, in which situation does the hypothesis \"$g'(x)\\neq0$ for all $x\\neq0$ sufficiently near $0$\" fail?","options":["$g'(1/n)=0$ for every positive integer $n$","$g'(x)=0$ only at the single point $x=2$","$g'(0)=0$, while $g'(x)\\neq0$ for every $x\\neq0$","$g'(x)<0$ for every $x\\neq0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ae40e582500f6dd5649d16d9","time_limit":40},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_l_h_pital_s_rule_add_002_curated20261027_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When L'Hôpital's rule is applied to $\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$, the limit of which expression is computed next?","options":["$\\dfrac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}$","$\\left(\\dfrac{f}{g}\\right)'(x)=\\dfrac{f'(x)g(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{g(x)^2}$","$\\dfrac{g'(x)}{f'(x)}$","$\\dfrac{f'(x)}{g(x)}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9bd7b8f3730af1650aa2ef3","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_l_h_pital_s_rule_add_004_curated20261027_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which limit has an indeterminate form that must first be rewritten as a quotient before L'Hôpital's rule can be used?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{x}$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\frac{x}{\\mathrm{e}^x}$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sin x\\cdot\\ln x$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{x}{\\sin(1/x)}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b093388fd912ee5c5e5dbc73","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_l_h_pital_s_rule_add_006_curated20261027_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^x-1}{x^2+x}$?","options":["$1$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$0$","It does not exist, since substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"531d61d28a3064fb596e62d4","time_limit":30}]}}